#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# euler_phi

#% text
# `euler_phi(n)` gives Euler's totient, the number of integers in `1 .. n` prime to `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `euler_phi` is multiplicative, with `euler_phi(p^k) = p^k - p^(k - 1)`; `euler_phi(1)` is 1.

#% item
# `euler_phi` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
euler_phi(12)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
euler_phi(13)
#% output
#= 12

#% cell
euler_phi(1)
#% output
#= 1

#% cell
euler_phi(range(10))
#% output
#= [1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 2, 6, 4, 6, 4]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A large argument, factored on the way:

#% cell
euler_phi(10^20 + 1)
#% output
#= 97910150554895155200

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The count by its definition:

#% cell
count_if(range(36), k => gcd(k, 36) == 1)
#% output
#= 12

#% cell
euler_phi(36)
#% output
#= 12

#% text
# The totients of the divisors add up to `n`:

#% cell
divisor_sum(60, euler_phi)
#% output
#= 60

#% text
# Euler's theorem `a^phi(n) = 1 mod n`:

#% cell
power_mod(7, euler_phi(40), 40)
#% output
#= 1

#% section
# See also

#% text
